%T A402229 87654320010,876543200001,8765432000000,87654311200000,
%U A402229 876543111120001,8765431111120000,87654311111112010,876543111111112001,8765431111111112000,87654311111111111210,876543111111111111201
%N A402229 a(n) is the largest n-digit number from which no earlier term can be obtained by deleting digits.
%C A402229 Deleting digits means removing some digits, adjacent or not; the remaining digits keep their order. For example, 877 can be obtained from 8767 by deleting the 6, but 88 cannot. In this entry, "x contains y" means that y can be obtained from x by deleting digits (or symbols, for strings), possibly none, so that every string contains itself. Thus a(n) is the largest n-digit number that contains none of a(1), ..., a(n-1), and the sequence stops at the first n for which every n-digit number contains an earlier term, so that a(n-1) is its last term.
%C A402229 The sequence is finite: by Higman's lemma (1952), every infinite sequence of strings over a finite set of symbols has a term that is contained in a later term, and the rule forbids this (the notes file also gives a direct proof). To say how long it is, let H_1(t) = 2^t and, for r >= 2, let H_r(t) be the result of applying H_(r-1) t times, starting from 1. So H_2(t) is a tower of t 2's, H_2(4) = 2^(2^(2^2)) = 65536 and H_3(4) = H_2(65536); in Knuth's up-arrow notation, H_r(t) is 2 followed by r arrows and t. The number N of terms satisfies H_7(10^127265) < N < H_8(12) (proved in the notes file; see the Formula section).
%C A402229 The same rule applies to strings over the b symbols 0, 1, ..., b-1, which may begin with 0: for k = 1, 2, 3, ..., the k-th string w_b(k) is the largest string of length k, in dictionary (lexicographic) order, that contains no earlier one, as long as there is such a string. Let L(b) be the number of strings obtained; for b <= 9, it is also the number of terms that begin with the first 10-b digits of 876543210 (see the Formula section). Here L(1) = 1, L(2) = 2 and L(3) = 6 (see the Example section), and L(4) = 5573661952...3106868300 has 127266 digits (computed; see the Links section).
%C A402229 L(b) grows very fast. Let M_b be the number of strings over 0, 1, ..., b-1, the empty string included, that contain none of w_b(1), ..., w_b(L(b)). Already L(5) > H_2(X) (proved), a tower of X 2's, where X = L(4) + M_4 + 1 has 254532 digits (computed); this is the case b = 5 of the bound with M_(b-1) in the Formula section. In general, L(b) eventually exceeds every primitive recursive function of b (proved), that is, every function built from 0, x+1 and the functions that return one of their arguments by composition and by recursion, in which f(0, y) = g(y) and f(x+1, y) = h(x, y, f(x, y)) for functions g and h already built. The Formula section compares L(b) with Ackermann's function.
%C A402229 Primitive recursive arithmetic is the formal system with the primitive recursive functions and induction only for statements without quantifiers. The single statement "for every b, the rule on b symbols stops" is true but cannot be proved in this system: a proof would carry over to a stronger system, where a theorem of Parsons (1970; see Buss) would give a primitive recursive f with f(b) > L(b) for every b, which is impossible. For each fixed b, including the decimal case, finiteness can be proved even in Robinson arithmetic, a very weak system, because the run of the rule is a finite computation, though for b >= 4 far too long to be carried out. Both statements are proved in the notes file.
%C A402229 The rule always takes the largest available string (a greedy rule), but this does not always give the longest list. Among all lists of strings over b symbols in which the i-th string has length i and no string contains an earlier one, the rule gives a longest one for b = 2 and b = 3 but not for b = 4, where another list has more than 2*10^180224 strings, and hence more than L(4) (proved in the notes file). By contrast, in an analogous problem for monomials (products of powers of variables) of prescribed consecutive degrees, where divisibility replaces containment, a greedy choice does give a longest sequence (Moreno Socías, 1991; see León Sánchez and Ovchinnikov, 2016, Proposition 2.3).
%C A402229 Long lists of strings in which no string contains an earlier one are classical: Friedman (2001) studied the longest ones when the i-th string has at most i+c symbols (c fixed), and Schmitz and Schnoebelen (2011) bounded their lengths when the length of the i-th string is limited by a given function of i. In A071062, the 26 minimal primes of Shallit, the same containment is applied to primes rather than to earlier terms: every prime contains one of them. A093382 and A094091 concern a related problem of Friedman on overlapping blocks of one binary string. The present rule was not found in the literature consulted. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
