%F A402229 Notation as in the Comments section; P_j is the first j digits of 876543210 (1 <= j <= 9). All statements of this section are proved in the notes file.
%F A402229 For b >= 2, w_b(1) is the single symbol b-1, and w_b(k+1) is the symbol b-2 followed by w_(b-1)(k) for 1 <= k <= L(b-1); hence L(b) > L(b-1). This gives the first L(b-1)+1 strings; the others (for b = 3: 0010, 00001, 000000) are given by an exact recursion in the notes file.
%F A402229 a(n) is P_j followed by w_(10-j)(n-j) for j+1 <= n <= j+L(10-j), and no other term begins with P_j; the last of these terms is P_j followed by L(10-j) zeros. For j <= 8, the range for j+1 lies inside the range for j, and on it the two descriptions agree by the formula for w_b(k+1) with b = 10-j. Every term after a(L(9)+1) begins with one of the digits 1, ..., 7.
%F A402229 a(n) is the string w_10(n), read as a number, and w_10(N+1) begins with 0; hence N < L(10).
%F A402229 a(N) = 10^(N-1).
%F A402229 For b >= 4, L(b) > H_(b-3)(L(b-1) + M_(b-1) + 1) > H_(b-3)(2*L(b-1)). For example, M_3 = 12 (the twelve strings in the Example section), so L(4) > H_1(19) = 2^19.
%F A402229 N > H_7(L(9) + M_9 + 1), because the proof of the bound with M_(b-1), for b = 10, counts only strings that come before w_10(N+1). As L(9) > L(5) > H_2(6) = 2^(2^65536) > 10^127265, this gives the lower bound for N in the Comments section.
%F A402229 L(4) < H_2(16), and L(b) < H_(b-2)(12) for b >= 5.
%F A402229 H_(b-3)(b-3) < L(b) for b >= 4, and L(b) < H_(b-2)(b-2) for b >= 14; so for b >= 14, L(b) lies between consecutive values of r -> H_r(r), a version of Ackermann's function. The upper inequality is false for b = 4, 5 and 6; whether it holds for 7 <= b <= 13 is not decided by the bounds given here.
%e A402229 a(1) = 9, the largest 1-digit number. a(2) = 88: each of 99, 98, ..., 89 contains 9, and 88 does not.
%e A402229 a(3) = 877: a 3-digit number greater than 877 either contains 9 or is one of 878, 880, 881, ..., 888, which contain 88 (in 878, delete the 7). The number 877 contains neither 9 nor 88.
%e A402229 a(4) = 8766: a 4-digit number greater than 8766 that contains neither 9 nor 88 is one of 8767, 8770, 8771, ..., 8777, which contain 877 (in 8767, delete the 6). The number 8766 contains none of 9, 88, 877.
%e A402229 For strings over b = 1 symbol the rule gives the single string 0 (the string 00 contains 0), and for b = 2 it gives 1, 00 (every string of length 3 over the symbols 0, 1 contains 1 or 00). So L(1) = 1 and L(2) = 2.
%e A402229 For b = 3 the rule gives 2, 11, 100, 0010, 00001, 000000, so L(3) = 6. After 2 and 11, a string may have no 2 and at most one 1, and 100 is the largest such string of length 3. Because of 100, a 1 may not be followed by two 0's, which leaves 0010 as the largest string of length 4; because of 0010, a 1 with two 0's before it may not have a 0 after it, which leaves 00001 at length 5; because of 00001, a 1 may not have four 0's before it, which leaves only 000000 at length 6.
%e A402229 At length 7, a string with one 1 has at least two 0's after the 1 (so it contains 100), or one 0 after it and five before it (so it contains 0010), or six 0's before it (so it contains 00001), and 0000000 contains 000000. So the rule stops.
%e A402229 The terms a(7), ..., a(14): the digits after the prefix 876543 are the strings w_4(1), ..., w_4(8), and for n = 8..13 the digits after the prefix 8765432 are the six strings w_3(1), ..., w_3(6), so that w_4(k+1) is 2 followed by w_3(k).
%e A402229    n   a(n)             after 876543   after 8765432
%e A402229    7   8765433          3
%e A402229    8   87654322         22             2
%e A402229    9   876543211        211            11
%e A402229   10   8765432100       2100           100
%e A402229   11   87654320010      20010          0010
%e A402229   12   876543200001     200001         00001
%e A402229   13   8765432000000    2000000        000000
%e A402229   14   87654311200000   11200000
%e A402229 The strings over 0, 1, 2 that contain none of 2, 11, 100, 0010, 00001, 000000 are the twelve strings 00000, 0001, 0000, 010, 001, 000, 10, 01, 00, 1, 0 and the empty string, listed longest first and, for equal lengths, in decreasing order. For n > 13, if a(n) begins with 876543, whatever follows a digit 2 after the prefix must be one of them, since otherwise a(n) would contain an earlier term.
%e A402229 For 14 <= n <= 25, a(n) is 876543, then a run of 1's, then 2, then the (n-13)-th of the twelve strings, the run of 1's being as long as needed for n digits (proved in the notes file): a(14) = 87654311200000 (876543, then 11, then 2, then 00000), ..., a(25) = 8765431111111111111111112. Then a(26) is 876543 followed by twenty 1's.
%o A402229 (Python)
%o A402229 # Descending depth-first search; reject a prefix containing an earlier term.
%o A402229 terms = []
%o A402229 def inside(u, v):
%o A402229     letters = iter(v)
%o A402229     return all(c in letters for c in u)
%o A402229 def largest(n, prefix=""):
%o A402229     if any(inside(t, prefix) for t in terms):
%o A402229         return None
%o A402229     if len(prefix) == n:
%o A402229         return prefix
%o A402229     for d in "9876543210":
%o A402229         if prefix or d != "0":
%o A402229             word = largest(n, prefix + d)
%o A402229             if word is not None:
%o A402229                 return word
%o A402229 for n in range(1, 31):
%o A402229     term = largest(n)
%o A402229     if term is None: break
%o A402229     terms.append(term)
%o A402229     print(term)
%o A402229 (Rust) // See Links section.
